# 
\title{L08.1 Introduction to Convnets}
\subtitle{CSci 560: Deep Learning w/ Python (Chollet) Ch. 8.1}
\maketitle

# The Convolution Operation

::: columns

::: {.column width=60%}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.25]{figures/ch08-1-2d-image-local-patterns}
\label{fig:ch08-1-2d-image-local-patterns}
\caption{Images can be broken into local patterns such as edges, textures, and so on. \parencite[pg.204]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

::::

:::: {.column width=40%}

The fundamental difference between a densley connected layer and a convolution
layer is this:

- `Dense` layers learn global patterns in their (flattened vector) input feature space
- `Conv2D` layers learn local patterns, in the case of images, patterns found in small 2D windows
   of the input.

::::

:::

# First Key Property of convnets - Translation-Invariance

The patterns that a convnet learns are **translation-invariant**.

- After learning a pattern in the lower-right corner, a convnet can recognize it anywhere.
- This makes convnets data-efficient because *the visual world is fundamentally translation
  invariant*.

# Second Key Property of convnets - Spatial Hierarchies

::: columns
::: {.column width=60%}


\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.18]{figures/ch08-2-spatial-hierarchy-of-patterns}
\label{fig:ch08-2-spatial-hierarchy-of-patterns}
\caption{The visual world forms a spatial hierarchy of visual modules:
elementary lines or textures combine into simple objects such as eyes
or ears, which combine into high-level concepts such as "cat". \parencite[pg.204]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

Convnets are capable of learning **spatial hierarchies** of patterns.

- A first convolutional layer will learn small patterns such as edges
- A second conv layer learns larger patterns made of the first layer patterns
  (like eyes or ears in the figure).

This allows convnets to efficiently learn increasingly complex and abstract visual
concepts because *the visual world is fundamentally spatially hierarchical*.
::::
:::

# The Convolution Operation (again)

- Convolutions operate over rank-3 tensors called **feature maps**
  - two spatial axes (height and width)
  - depth axis
- Convolution operation extracts patches (`kernel_size`) and applies same transformation
  to all these patches.
- The output feature map is a rank-3 tensor
  - it has wiedth and height
  - Its depth is arbitrary (`filters`)
- **Filters** encode specific aspects of the input data, and detect it in a patch or not.
- At a high level a single filter could encode the concept "presence of a face"

# MNIST Convolutions

::: columns
::: {.column width=40%}
- First convolution layer takes input feature map of size `(28, 28, 1)`
- Outputs feature map of size `(26, 26, 32)`
- It computes 32 filters over its input.
- Each of these 32 output channels contains a 26x26 grid of values
  - which is a **respnse map** of the filter over the input, indicating response
    of that pattern at image locations.
::::
:::: {.column width=60%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.24]{figures/ch08-3-response-map}
\label{fig:ch08-3-response-map}
\caption{The concept of a response map: a 2D map of the presence of a pattern
at different locations in an input. \parencite[pg.204]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::

# Feature Maps

- **Feature map**: every dimension in the depth axis is a *feature*
  or *filter*
- The rank-2 tensor `output[:, :, n]` is the 2D spatial *map* of the response of this
  filter over the input.

Convolutions are defined by two key parameters:

1. Size of the patches extracted from inputs - typically 3x3 or 5x5.
2. Depth of the output feature map - number of filters/features discovered by the convolution.

# How Convolution Works

::: columns
::: {.column width=50%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.15]{figures/ch08-4-how-convolution-works}
\label{fig:ch08-4-how-convolution-works}
\caption{How convolution works. \parencite[pg.207]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
- Slide window (e.g. 3x3) over the 3D input feature map.
- Each such 3D patch is transformed into a 1D vector
  - by tensor product of **convolution kernel** with the patch
- All vectors (one per patch) are spatially reassembled into 3D output map

Note: output widthxheight is 3x3 because of the sliding
::::
:::

# Understanding Border Effects

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.25]{figures/ch08-5-sliding}
\label{fig:ch08-5-sliding}
\caption{Valid locations of 3x3 patches in a 5x5 input feature map. \parencite[pg.208]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

# Padding and Striding

- **Padding** consists of adding an appropriate number of rows and columns on each side of the input 
  feature map so as to fit center convolution windows around every input tile.
- **Strided convolutions**: distance between two successive windows 
  - Using stride 2 means the width and height of the feature map are downsampled by a factor of 2
- In classification models, instead of strides, tend to use the **max-pooling** operation to
  downsample feature maps.
 
# Add Padding to Keep Spatial Dimensions

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.25]{figures/ch08-6-padding}
\label{fig:ch08-6-padding}
\caption{Padding a 5x5 input in order to be able to extract 25 3x3 patches. \parencite[pg.208]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

# Understanding Convolution Strides

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.25]{figures/ch08-7-strides}
\label{fig:ch08-7-strides}  
\caption{3x3 convolution patches with 2x2 strides. \parencite[pg.209]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

# The Max-pooling Operation

- You may have noticed that the size of the feature maps is halved after every `MaxPooling2D` layer.
- The role of max pooling: to aggressively downsample feature maps.
- Similar to convolutions, slide windows and transform local patches.  But a simple `max` is done
  rather than a tensor transformation 
- Usually use max pooling with 2x2 window and stride 2.
- Usually use convolution with 3x3 window and no stride (stride 1).

# Summary of Convnets, Convolutions and Max Pooling

- The visual world is fundamentally **translation-invariant**, convolutions learn translation-invariant kernels.
- The visual world is fundamentally **spatially hierarchical**, successive convoluton layers are capable of learning increasingly high-level spatial herarchies.
- Convoultion layers operate by sliding a window to extract patches, and applies convolution kernel learned to each pass.
- Every dimension in the depth axis from a convolution is a **feature map**.
- The role of max pooling is to aggressively downsample feature maps.

